data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Del"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Del"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; rep"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; rep"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do.\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do.\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also,"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also,"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do.\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\Delta x| \\neq 0$ for"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\\\Delta x| \\\\neq 0$ for"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do.\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\Delta x| \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$."},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\\\Delta x| \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$."}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do.\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\Delta x| \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$.\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\Delta u \\"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\\\Delta x| \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$.\\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do.\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\Delta x| \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$.\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\Delta u \\neq 0$ always. Growth rate is"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\\\Delta x| \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$.\\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$ always. Growth rate is"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do.\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\Delta x| \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$.\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\Delta u \\neq 0$ always. Growth rate is irrelevant.\n\nThe rigorous pro"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\\\Delta x| \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$.\\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$ always. Growth rate is irrelevant.\\n\\nThe rigorous pro"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01E8jLBerBY9ExSTCKc8PHHA","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\n$$\\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{f(g(x+\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\cdot\\frac{g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\Delta x}$$\nand lets $\\Delta x \\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?","options":[{"label":"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$","value":"cube"},{"label":"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$","value":"abs"},{"label":"$g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$","value":"osc"},{"label":"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial","value":"exp"}],"details":"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?","explanation":"The step divides by $g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\Delta x = \\frac{1}{n\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\n\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\Delta x^3 \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\Delta u$ do.\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\Delta x| \\neq 0$ for every $\\Delta x \\neq 0$.\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\Delta u \\neq 0$ always. Growth rate is irrelevant.\n\nThe rigorous proof handles this case by replacing the first ratio with a function that equ"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\\\Delta x| \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$.\\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$ always. Growth rate is irrelevant.\\n\\nThe rigorous proof handles this case by replacing the first ratio with a function that equ"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":9225,"totalTokens":18692,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":9225},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860805909,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQmk5BKFnxTWtBudKoB"}}

