Delta u ="},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"To prove the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$, someone writes\\n$$\\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{f(g(x+\\\\Delta x)) - f(g(x))}{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)}{\\\\Delta x}$$\\nand lets $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$. For which inner function $g$ does this step break down near $x = 0$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^3$, because $g'(0) = 0$\",\"value\":\"cube\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = |x|$, because $g$ has a corner at $0$\",\"value\":\"abs\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ with $g(0)=0$, because $g$ returns to $g(0)$ at points arbitrarily close to $0$\",\"value\":\"osc\"},{\"label\":\"$g(x) = e^x$, because $g$ grows faster than any polynomial\",\"value\":\"exp\"}], \"details\": \"Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field. What specifically goes wrong in the rewritten expression?\", \"explanation\": \"The step divides by $g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x)$. That is only legal when this difference is nonzero. For $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$, the difference is $0$ at $\\\\Delta x = \\\\frac{1}{n\\\\pi}$ for every integer $n$, so it hits zero at points arbitrarily close to $0$. No matter how small a window around $0$ you choose, the expression is undefined somewhere inside it, and the limit argument fails.\\n\\n- $x^3$ has $g'(0) = 0$, but $g(\\\\Delta x) - g(0) = \\\\Delta x^3 \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$, so the division is fine. A zero derivative does not cause the problem; repeated zeros of $\\\\Delta u$ do.\\n- $|x|$ isn't differentiable at $0$, so the chain rule doesn't claim anything there. Also, $|\\\\Delta x| \\\\neq 0$ for every $\\\\Delta x \\\\neq 0$.\\n- $e^x$ is strictly increasing, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$ always. Growth rate is irrelevant.\\n\\nThe rigorous proof handles this case by replacing the first ratio with a function that equals $f'(g(x))$ whenever $\\\\Delta u = "}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","